Finally, multi-tenancy is handled with explicit pool boundaries and quotas. In shared pools, tenants get capped burst budgets and are quarantined on anomaly (sudden unknown-user/complaint spikes) so one compromised account can’t torch the pool. Dedicated pools get more freedom, but still run through the same shaping logic—because “dedicated” doesn’t mean “immune to bad behavior.”

## Trade-offs

| Optimized For | Sacrificed |
|--------------|------------|
| Deliverability stability and fast recovery | Raw peak throughput under heavy throttling |
| Operational debuggability (single lifecycle ledger) | Some extra write amplification (events + index) |
| Tenant isolation via pool boundaries | Slightly slower onboarding (warmup) and more IP management |

## Failure Modes

- **Provider throttling cascade (mass 4xx deferrals)**
  - What happens: queues grow, retries amplify load, MTAs churn connections, p99 latency spikes.
  - Detect: per-domain deferral rate + queue age + connection error rate.
  - Recover: reputation engine clamps concurrency, increases retry delays with jitter, temporarily reroutes to healthier pools only when safe (not as a default).

- **Complaint spike from a single tenant (abuse or bad list)**
  - What happens: shared pool reputation degrades; inbox placement drops broadly.
