-- **10x scale:** Split the reputation engine into per-provider domain controllers (Gmail/Yahoo/Outlook) to reduce blast radius and tune logic independently; introduce regional MTA POPs to keep provider connections warm and latency stable.
-- **100x scale:** Move from “one global event log + derived indexes” to regionally-sharded logs with local suppression caches and strict replication semantics; dedicate teams to provider-specific deliverability programs and automated remediation workflows.
+- **10x scale:** Split budgets by provider family (Gmail/Yahoo/Outlook) and deploy regional MTA POPs to keep connections warm.
+- **100x scale:** Regionalize the lifecycle DB and suppression with explicit replication semantics; dedicate provider-specific deliverability automation.
 
 ## Operational Notes
 
 - The only numbers that matter on-call are per-domain deferrals, complaint rate, and unknown-user rate; CPU graphs are usually a distraction during incidents.
-- Always keep a replay path: when (not if) bounce classifications change, you need to recompute suppression decisions and reputation features from the event log.
-- Automate quarantine and kill-switches; humans are too slow when a shared pool starts burning.
-- Treat suppression lookups as part of the send SLO: if suppression is slow or stale, you will send to bad addresses and pay for it in reputation.
